(window.DOM_CHAPTERS=window.DOM_CHAPTERS||{})["shock"]="<h1>Circulatory Shock</h1>\n<h2 id=\"1-what-is-shock\"><span class=\"sec-num\">1.</span> What is shock?</h2>\n<p><strong>Shock</strong> is a failure of the circulation to deliver enough blood to the tissues to keep them working normally. If it goes on long enough, the tissues are <strong>damaged</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Shock is not defined by a blood pressure number.</strong> Recall <strong><span data-term=\"map\">mean arterial pressure</span> ≈ cardiac output × total peripheral resistance</strong>. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/blood-pressure\">→ Blood Pressure</a>)</em> Early in shock, the body constricts its arterioles hard, so blood pressure can look <strong>nearly normal</strong> while cardiac output, and the flow to many organs, has already fallen.</p>\n<p>Every type of shock comes down to one of four failures:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Type</th>\n  <th>What has failed</th>\n  <th>Examples (from the lecture)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Hypovolaemic</strong> (low volume)</td>\n  <td>Not enough <strong>blood volume</strong></td>\n  <td>Haemorrhage, trauma, surgery, <strong>burns</strong>, severe <strong>diarrhoea and vomiting</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Cardiogenic</strong></td>\n  <td>The heart can't <strong>pump</strong></td>\n  <td>Myocardial infarction, heart failure, arrhythmias</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Distributive</strong> (low resistance)</td>\n  <td>Vessels <strong>dilate</strong> massively, so blood pools and is maldistributed</td>\n  <td><strong>Septic</strong> shock, <strong>anaphylaxis</strong>, <strong>neurogenic</strong> shock</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Obstructive</strong></td>\n  <td>Blood flow is <strong>blocked</strong></td>\n  <td>Cardiac tamponade, <span data-term=\"tension-pneumothorax\">tension pneumothorax</span>, pulmonary embolism, cardiac tumour</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2 id=\"2-haemorrhage-the-model-for-all-shock\"><span class=\"sec-num\">2.</span> Haemorrhage: the model for all shock</h2>\n<p>The lecture uses <strong>haemorrhage</strong> to illustrate the events in every kind of shock. Follow it link by link:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Blood is lost</strong>, so <strong>blood volume falls</strong>.</li>\n<li>Less blood fills the veins, so <strong><span data-term=\"venous-return\">venous return</span> falls</strong>. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/venous-return\">→ Veins and Venous Return</a>)</em></li>\n<li>Less filling, so <strong><span data-term=\"stroke-volume\">stroke volume</span></strong> falls (Frank–Starling), and <strong>cardiac output falls</strong>. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-output\">→ Cardiac Output</a>)</em></li>\n<li>Blood pressure falls, and the <strong>tissues are underperfused</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Everything else, the signs, the compensation and the deterioration, follows from this chain.</p>\n<h2 id=\"3-what-you-see-and-why\"><span class=\"sec-num\">3.</span> What you see, and why</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Sign</th>\n  <th>Why it happens</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Low blood pressure</strong></td>\n  <td>Low cardiac output. (Early on, constriction may hide it.)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Fast, weak pulse</strong></td>\n  <td><span data-term=\"baroreceptors\">Baroreceptors</span> fire less, so the <span data-term=\"vasomotor-centre\">vasomotor centre</span> is released: sympathetic drive gives <strong>reflex tachycardia</strong>. The pulse is <strong>weak</strong> because <span data-term=\"stroke-volume\">stroke volume</span> is small. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/reflexes?section=3-the-baroreflex-step-by-step\">→ Reflexes, §3</a>)</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Pale, cool, clammy skin</strong></td>\n  <td>Sympathetic <strong>vasoconstriction</strong> is generalised but most <strong>marked in the skin</strong>; sweating adds the clamminess. The <strong>heart and brain are spared</strong>. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/skin?section=2-skin-blood-flow-in-shock\">→ Cutaneous Circulation, §2</a>)</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Low urine output</strong></td>\n  <td>Constriction of renal arterioles lowers <strong>GFR</strong>; the kidneys also keep <strong>Na⁺</strong> and water. Urea builds up in the blood.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Rapid breathing</strong></td>\n  <td>Low flow starves the <strong>chemoreceptors</strong> of oxygen, and <strong>lactic acid</strong> builds up; both drive breathing. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/reflexes?section=5-chemoreceptors\">→ Reflexes, §5</a>)</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Intense thirst</strong></td>\n  <td><strong><span data-term=\"angiotensin-ii\">Angiotensin II</span></strong> and <strong><span data-term=\"adh\">ADH</span></strong> rise.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Restless and anxious</strong>, later <strong>dull and apathetic</strong></td>\n  <td>Early: sympathetic arousal (restlessness also works the <strong>muscle pump</strong>). Later: <strong>cerebral ischaemia</strong>.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Lactic acidosis</strong></td>\n  <td>Without enough oxygen, cells switch to <strong>anaerobic glycolysis</strong>, making <strong>lactic acid</strong>.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2 id=\"4-how-the-body-fights-back\"><span class=\"sec-num\">4.</span> How the body fights back</h2>\n<p><strong>Within seconds: nerves.</strong> Falling blood pressure unloads the <span data-term=\"baroreceptors\">baroreceptors</span>, and the chemoreceptors are stimulated. Sympathetic discharge rises:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Heart:</strong> faster and stronger.</li>\n<li><strong>Arterioles:</strong> constrict everywhere <strong>except the heart and brain</strong>, raising resistance and redirecting flow to them.</li>\n<li><strong>Veins:</strong> constrict, squeezing out stored blood. The <strong>splanchnic</strong> veins, the <strong>skin</strong> and even the <strong>pulmonary</strong> veins give up blood to the systemic circulation. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/splanchnic?section=3-blood-flow-after-meals-and-in-shock\">→ Splanchnic Circulation, §3</a>)</em></li>\n<li><strong>Adrenal medulla:</strong> releases <strong>adrenaline and noradrenaline</strong>, reinforcing all of this.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Within minutes to hours: hormones.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Renin → angiotensin II → <span data-term=\"aldosterone\">aldosterone</span>:</strong> constriction, salt and water retention, thirst.</li>\n<li><strong><span data-term=\"adh\">ADH (vasopressin)</span>:</strong> water retention and constriction.</li>\n<li><strong>ACTH → cortisol.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/neural-humoral?section=6-fast-slow-and-slower-putting-it-together\">→ Neural and Hormonal Control, §6</a>)</em></p>\n<p><strong>Within hours: fluid moves into the blood.</strong> Arteriolar constriction and low pressure <strong>lower capillary <span data-term=\"hydrostatic-pressure\">hydrostatic pressure</span></strong>, so the balance at the capillaries tips towards <strong>reabsorption</strong>. Fluid moves from the <strong>interstitial spaces into the blood</strong>, partly refilling it. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/microcirculation\">→ Microcirculation</a>)</em></p>\n<p><strong>Over days to weeks: full repair.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>If bleeding stops, <strong>blood volume</strong> is restored in about <strong>12–72 hours</strong>, mainly by drawing in fluid and retaining salt and water.</li>\n<li>That added fluid <strong>dilutes</strong> the blood, so <strong>plasma proteins</strong> and <strong>haemoglobin</strong> concentration fall.</li>\n<li><strong>Erythropoietin</strong> from the kidney drives new red cell production; haemoglobin returns to normal in about <strong>4–5 weeks</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>The lecture's question: Hb and haematocrit after losing whole blood</h3>\n<p><strong>Immediately after a bleed, Hb and <span data-term=\"haematocrit\">haematocrit</span> are normal.</strong> Whole blood is lost, so red cells and plasma are lost <strong>in the same proportion</strong>: what remains has the same concentration. They <strong>fall later</strong>, as interstitial fluid (and any IV fluid) dilutes the remaining red cells. So a <strong>normal Hb doesn't rule out a large, recent bleed</strong>.</p>\n<section class=\"check-block\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"check-launch\" aria-haspopup=\"dialog\" data-questions=\"3\" data-part=\"Sections 1–4\"><span class=\"cl-badge\" aria-hidden=\"true\">?</span><span class=\"cl-text\"><strong>Understanding Check</strong><span class=\"cl-sub\">Sections 1–4 · 3 questions</span></span><span class=\"cl-go\">Start</span></button><div class=\"check-body\"><p class=\"check-label\"><strong>Understanding Check: Sections 1–4</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1.</strong> Why can blood pressure look nearly normal early in shock?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Cardiac output rises early in shock, offsetting the fall in volume</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. The blood becomes more viscous, raising resistance and pressure</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Shock affects only the veins, not the arteries, at first</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Sympathetic constriction raises resistance, hiding a fall in cardiac output</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-qid=\"shock:262dfb4b\" data-answer=\"D\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: D.</em> MAP ≈ CO × TPR. A rising TPR can hold MAP up while CO and organ flow fall.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2.</strong> Why is the skin pale and cool in haemorrhagic shock, while the brain stays perfused?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. The skin loses blood first, because its vessels are closest to the wound</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Sympathetic constriction is marked in the skin but spares the heart and brain</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. The brain constricts its own arteries to hold blood inside the skull</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Low body temperature constricts the skin vessels in all patients</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-qid=\"shock:e7896f5b\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> Generalised constriction redirects flow to the heart and brain, which have little sympathetic constriction.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>3.</strong> A man has lost 1.5 L of blood in the last 30 minutes. His haemoglobin is normal. Why?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. The spleen has released enough stored red cells to replace those lost</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Erythropoietin has already restored the red cell count</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Red cells and plasma were lost together, so the concentration hasn't changed yet</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. The kidneys have retained enough water to restore the concentration</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-qid=\"shock:dc885532\" data-answer=\"C\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: C.</em> Whole blood loss keeps the ratio the same. Hb falls later, as fluid moves in and dilutes the blood.</p></details></div></section>\n<h2 id=\"5-when-shock-feeds-on-itself-progressive-and-irreversible-shock\"><span class=\"sec-num\">5.</span> When shock feeds on itself: progressive and irreversible shock</h2>\n<p>If the blood loss is large or prolonged, the compensation fails, and several <strong>vicious cycles</strong> (positive feedback) take over. Each one makes perfusion worse, which makes the cycle turn faster:</p>\n<figure class=\"learning-figure shock-cycle-figure\" id=\"cvs20-cycle\"><figcaption>Progressive shock: the vicious cycles</figcaption><svg viewBox=\"0 0 400 400\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"A central box, low cardiac output and blood pressure, with four loops that each lead back to it. One: low coronary flow and acidosis weaken the heart. Two: the vasomotor centre fails, so the heart slows and vessels dilate. Three: lactic acidosis depresses the heart and the vessels stop responding to adrenaline. Four: capillary stasis makes fluid leak out, so volume falls. Each loop lowers cardiac output and blood pressure further.\"><defs><marker id=\"sh-head\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"8\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"6\" markerHeight=\"6\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0 0L10 5L0 10z\" class=\"sh-arrowhead\"/></marker></defs><g class=\"sh-arrow\"><path d=\"M122 168L68 104\" marker-end=\"url(#sh-head)\"/><path d=\"M140 104L176 166\" marker-end=\"url(#sh-head)\"/><path d=\"M278 168L332 104\" marker-end=\"url(#sh-head)\"/><path d=\"M260 104L224 166\" marker-end=\"url(#sh-head)\"/><path d=\"M122 234L68 304\" marker-end=\"url(#sh-head)\"/><path d=\"M140 304L176 238\" marker-end=\"url(#sh-head)\"/><path d=\"M278 234L332 304\" marker-end=\"url(#sh-head)\"/><path d=\"M260 304L224 238\" marker-end=\"url(#sh-head)\"/></g><rect x=\"100\" y=\"170\" width=\"200\" height=\"64\" rx=\"12\" class=\"sh-centre\"/><g class=\"sh-box\"><rect x=\"8\" y=\"16\" width=\"176\" height=\"86\" rx=\"10\"/><rect x=\"216\" y=\"16\" width=\"176\" height=\"86\" rx=\"10\"/><rect x=\"8\" y=\"306\" width=\"176\" height=\"86\" rx=\"10\"/><rect x=\"216\" y=\"306\" width=\"176\" height=\"86\" rx=\"10\"/></g><g text-anchor=\"middle\"><text x=\"200\" y=\"196\" class=\"sh-lab\">Low cardiac output</text><text x=\"200\" y=\"216\" class=\"sh-lab\">and blood pressure</text><text x=\"96\" y=\"40\" class=\"sh-lab\">1 · Heart weakens</text><text x=\"96\" y=\"60\" class=\"sh-sub\">low coronary flow</text><text x=\"96\" y=\"78\" class=\"sh-sub\">and acidosis</text><text x=\"304\" y=\"40\" class=\"sh-lab\">2 · Brainstem fails</text><text x=\"304\" y=\"60\" class=\"sh-sub\">vasomotor centre: heart</text><text x=\"304\" y=\"78\" class=\"sh-sub\">slows, vessels dilate</text><text x=\"96\" y=\"330\" class=\"sh-lab\">3 · Lactic acidosis</text><text x=\"96\" y=\"350\" class=\"sh-sub\">heart depressed; vessels</text><text x=\"96\" y=\"368\" class=\"sh-sub\">ignore adrenaline</text><text x=\"304\" y=\"330\" class=\"sh-lab\">4 · Capillary stasis</text><text x=\"304\" y=\"350\" class=\"sh-sub\">blood stagnates, fluid</text><text x=\"304\" y=\"368\" class=\"sh-sub\">leaks: volume falls</text></g></svg><p>Each loop starts from poor perfusion and ends by lowering cardiac output and blood pressure further, so the cycle turns faster. </p></figure>\n\n<ol>\n<li><strong>The heart fails.</strong> Low blood pressure means low <strong>coronary</strong> flow. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/coronary?section=3-why-is-left-ventricular-coronary-perfusion-greatest-during-diastole\">→ Coronary Circulation, §3</a>)</em> Ischaemia and <strong>acidosis</strong> weaken the heart muscle, so cardiac output falls further.</li>\n<li><strong>The vasomotor centre fails.</strong> Prolonged <strong>cerebral ischaemia</strong> depresses it. Sympathetic drive collapses: the heart <strong>slows</strong> and the vessels <strong>dilate</strong>. Blood pressure and brain flow fall further.</li>\n<li><strong>Acidosis.</strong> <strong>Lactic acid</strong> depresses the heart and makes the vessels <strong>stop responding to adrenaline and noradrenaline</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Capillary stasis and leak.</strong> Late in shock, the <strong><span data-term=\"precapillary-sphincter\">precapillary sphincters</span> relax but the venules stay constricted</strong>. Blood enters the capillaries but can't leave, so it <strong>stagnates</strong>, capillary <strong><span data-term=\"hydrostatic-pressure\">hydrostatic pressure</span> rises</strong>, and fluid is <strong>lost into the tissues</strong>. Volume falls further.</li>\n<li><strong>The kidneys are damaged.</strong> Prolonged low flow can cause <strong>acute tubular damage</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Irreversible shock:</strong> eventually, even if the lost blood is <strong>fully replaced</strong>, the circulation doesn't recover: resistance stays low, the heart stays weak, and the patient dies. The tissues, especially the heart and brain, have been too badly damaged.</p>\n<h2 id=\"6-the-other-types-of-shock\"><span class=\"sec-num\">6.</span> The other types of shock</h2>\n<p><strong>Hypovolaemic shock without visible bleeding.</strong> Bleeding can be <strong>concealed</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>A <strong>fractured femur</strong> can hide over a litre of blood in the thigh.</li>\n<li>A <strong>ruptured spleen</strong> or a ruptured <strong>ectopic pregnancy</strong> bleeds into the abdomen.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Other causes of volume loss (the lecture's list):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Burns:</strong> <strong>plasma</strong> seeps from the burnt skin, but the red cells stay behind. So the blood becomes <strong>concentrated</strong> (haemoconcentration): Hb and <span data-term=\"haematocrit\">haematocrit</span> <strong>rise</strong>, the opposite of haemorrhage.</li>\n<li><strong>Traumatic shock:</strong> crushed muscle releases <strong>myoglobin</strong>, which damages the kidneys.</li>\n<li><strong>Surgical shock:</strong> bleeding and dehydration.</li>\n<li><strong>Diarrhoea, uncontrolled diabetes and adrenal insufficiency:</strong> salt and water loss.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Cardiogenic shock.</strong> The volume is normal, but the heart can't pump it, so cardiac output falls (myocardial infarction, heart failure, arrhythmias). Blood <strong>backs up</strong> behind the failing ventricle, so venous pressure and the <span data-term=\"jvp\">JVP</span> are often <strong>high</strong>, unlike haemorrhage. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/heart-failure\">→ Heart Failure</a>)</em></p>\n<p><strong>Distributive (low-resistance) shock.</strong> The blood volume is normal, but <strong>massive vasodilation</strong> lets blood <strong>pool</strong> and flow is maldistributed:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Type</th>\n  <th>Cause</th>\n  <th>Key feature</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Neurogenic</strong></td>\n  <td>Sudden loss of <span data-term=\"sympathetic-tone\">sympathetic tone</span> (spinal cord injury, spinal anaesthesia; the lecture also includes fainting from strong emotion)</td>\n  <td>Vessels dilate; the heart may be <strong>slow</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Septic</strong></td>\n  <td><strong>Bacterial toxins</strong></td>\n  <td>Vasodilation <strong>and</strong> leaky capillaries; skin may be <strong>warm</strong> early <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/skin?section=2-skin-blood-flow-in-shock\">→ Cutaneous Circulation, §2</a>)</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Anaphylactic</strong></td>\n  <td>An <strong>antigen–antibody</strong> reaction releases <strong>histamine</strong></td>\n  <td>Widespread dilation <strong>and</strong> leaky capillaries</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Obstructive shock.</strong> Something blocks filling or outflow: fluid compressing the heart (<strong><span data-term=\"tamponade\">tamponade</span></strong>), a <strong>tension pneumothorax</strong> squeezing the great veins, or a <strong>pulmonary embolism</strong> blocking the pulmonary artery.</p>\n<section class=\"check-block\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"check-launch\" aria-haspopup=\"dialog\" data-questions=\"4\" data-part=\"Sections 5–6\"><span class=\"cl-badge\" aria-hidden=\"true\">?</span><span class=\"cl-text\"><strong>Understanding Check</strong><span class=\"cl-sub\">Sections 5–6 · 4 questions</span></span><span class=\"cl-go\">Start</span></button><div class=\"check-body\"><p class=\"check-label\"><strong>Understanding Check: Sections 5–6</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1.</strong> In progressive shock, why does failure of the vasomotor centre make things worse?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Sympathetic drive collapses, so the heart slows and the vessels dilate</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. The vasomotor centre starts releasing more adrenaline into the blood</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. The baroreceptors stop firing, so blood pressure rises suddenly</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Breathing stops, so the lungs no longer supply oxygen</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-qid=\"shock:94f6a1cf\" data-answer=\"A\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: A.</em> Prolonged cerebral ischaemia depresses the vasomotor centre, removing the constriction that was holding up pressure.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2.</strong> Late in shock, the precapillary sphincters relax while the venules stay constricted. What follows?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Blood flows faster, washing out the lactic acid from the tissues</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Capillary pressure falls, so fluid moves back into the blood</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. The venules pump blood back to the heart more efficiently</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Blood stagnates, capillary pressure rises and fluid leaks out</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-qid=\"shock:cec7d696\" data-answer=\"D\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: D.</em> Blood enters the capillaries but can't leave, so filtration rises and more volume is lost.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>3.</strong> A patient with severe burns has a raised haematocrit. Why is this different from haemorrhage?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Burns lose red cells but not plasma, so the blood becomes diluted</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Burns stimulate erythropoietin at once, so new red cells appear</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Burns lose plasma but not red cells, so the blood becomes concentrated</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Burns lose whole blood, which always raises the haematocrit</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-qid=\"shock:7f96efaf\" data-answer=\"C\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: C.</em> Plasma seeps from the burnt skin while red cells stay behind: haemoconcentration.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>4.</strong> How does septic shock differ from haemorrhagic shock in its early stage?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Volume is low and vessels constrict, so the skin is always cold</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Volume is normal but vessels dilate and leak, so the skin may be warm</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. The heart stops pumping, so blood backs up into the veins</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. A blood clot blocks the pulmonary artery, so filling falls</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-qid=\"shock:6b1b37e2\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> Bacterial toxins cause vasodilation and leaky capillaries: a distributive shock.</p></details></div></section>\n<h2 id=\"7-chapter-essentials\"><span class=\"sec-num\">7.</span> Chapter Essentials</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Shock</strong> = tissue perfusion too low for normal function, which damages tissues. Blood pressure can look normal early.</li>\n<li><strong>Four types:</strong> hypovolaemic (volume), cardiogenic (pump), distributive (vessels dilate), obstructive (flow blocked).</li>\n<li><strong>Haemorrhage chain:</strong> volume ↓ → venous return ↓ → stroke volume ↓ → cardiac output ↓ → perfusion ↓.</li>\n<li><strong>Signs:</strong> low BP; fast, weak pulse; pale, cool skin; low urine; rapid breathing; thirst; restless, then dull; lactic acidosis. Each has a mechanism.</li>\n<li><strong>Compensation:</strong> baroreflex and chemoreceptors (seconds), adrenal medulla, renin–angiotensin–aldosterone, ADH, cortisol (minutes to hours), interstitial fluid moving into the blood (hours).</li>\n<li><strong>Repair:</strong> volume in 12–72 h; Hb by erythropoietin in 4–5 weeks. <strong>Hb is normal straight after a bleed</strong>, then falls with dilution.</li>\n<li><strong>Progressive shock:</strong> heart fails, vasomotor centre fails, acidosis, capillary stasis and leak, kidney damage. <strong>Irreversible shock:</strong> no recovery even when volume is replaced.</li>\n<li><strong>Burns</strong> concentrate the blood (plasma loss); <strong>trauma</strong> releases myoglobin; bleeding can be <strong>concealed</strong> (femur, spleen, ectopic).</li>\n<li><strong>Distributive:</strong> neurogenic, septic (warm skin early), anaphylactic (histamine). <strong>Cardiogenic:</strong> high venous pressure. <strong>Obstructive:</strong> tamponade, tension pneumothorax, pulmonary embolism.</li>\n</ol>\n";
